Your more important move is exactly right: stop admiring the new mathematics and start spending it.

The highest-value new application family I see is a Novel Application Foundry built from:

\text{Same}+\text{Different}+\text{Again}
+\text{Numantics}
+\text{Retroaxiomatic discovery}

Its job is:

\boxed{
\text{find an invariant somewhere}
\rightarrow
\text{extract it}
\rightarrow
\text{search for alien domains where it should work}
\rightarrow
\text{instantiate}
\rightarrow
\text{test}
}

That produces several immediately useful machines.

1. Cross-Domain Rhyme Search. Give it a mechanism—say Git branching—and instead of searching for things that look like Git, search for systems sharing its invariant:

\text{state}+\text{branch}+\text{delta}+\text{merge}

That may uncover applications in strategy, drug development, negotiations, narrative design, organizational planning, or evolutionary search. This is search by mathematical rhyme rather than vocabulary.

2. Broken-Rhyme Radar. Establish the normal relational pattern in any system and look for where it stops holding:

\text{expected invariant}-\text{observed relation}

Applications: software bugs, fraud, mechanical failure, market regime changes, scientific anomalies, inconsistent arguments, bad assumptions. Instead of generic anomaly detection, it can explain which rhyme broke.

3. Semantic Goal Seek++. Ordinary optimization searches numbers. Ours can search representations and interventions:

\text{desired outcome}
\rightarrow
\text{what relations must hold?}
\rightarrow
\text{what variables can move?}
\rightarrow
\text{what smallest intervention changes the trajectory?}

This could be used on businesses, schedules, negotiations, software architectures, game strategies, policy models—anywhere the desired endpoint is clearer than the route.

4. Retroaxiomatic Scientist. Feed it observations without telling it the theory. It repeatedly asks:

\text{What is Same?}

\text{What is Different?}

\text{What happens Again?}

Then it proposes the smallest generative rule explaining the observations and deliberately predicts unseen cases. That turns our playful derivation style into an actual scientific-discovery loop.

5. Operator Fossil Hunter. Mine mature software, mathematics, biological mechanisms, games, rituals, stories, and crafts for hidden reusable operators. Excel yields dependency propagation and backward solving; Git yields branching provenance; music yields counterpoint and cadence; biology yields homeostasis and adaptation. The output isn’t an analogy—it is an extracted, testable computational skeleton.

6. Representation Roulette. When a problem appears random or stuck, systematically transform it:

x
\rightarrow\Delta x
\rightarrow\Delta^2x
\rightarrow\text{ratios}
\rightarrow\text{graph}
\rightarrow\text{symmetry}
\rightarrow\text{roles}
\rightarrow\text{causal structure}

and ask at every stage:

Does it rhyme now?

That could become one of Pantheon’s strongest general problem-solving routines.

And there is an especially interesting seventh application: discovering mathematics itself.

Take a newly noticed pattern P. Instead of asking only “what existing mathematics describes this?”, run:

P
\rightarrow
\text{candidate invariant}
\rightarrow
\text{minimal axioms}
\rightarrow
\text{closure under composition}
\rightarrow
\text{edge cases}
\rightarrow
\text{counterexamples}
\rightarrow
\text{theorems}

That’s our retroaxiomatic route made explicit.

So the new mathematics can begin generating more mathematics.

And your sleight-of-hand / Rock Lee point belongs here too. A feint is basically a representation attack:

